Z||000002||The Fieromaster||07-17-2001||09:47 PM||||Axel seals huh??  BE CAREFUL! My axle seal went and i didnt know it and it took my syncros and $hit out!!  Bad things!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>------------------<BR>      <FONT COLOR="red">"Raced That, Wrecked Better"</FONT><BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.askfelons.com/fieromaster/fieromastergif1.gif">        <BR><B>~In Memory Of Jesse Cesek~ 1980-2000</B><BR><FONT COLOR="blue"><B>AIM: Fieromaster</B></FONT><BR><B><FONT COLOR="purple">1985 GT ~ Purple, Black and Yellow Interior(Fastback Converted)</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="maroon">1997 Mitsu Eclipse GSX ~ Maroon w/ Hella Balls!(GSX = AWD, Turbo) DOA on 12/10/2000</FONT></B>||||reg||11
Z||000003||California Kid||07-17-2001||10:10 PM||||If you plan on keeping that car a long time, you should try to get you money back for the seals and get the Axle Stabilizer Needle Bearing with Seal build in. This also replaces the "probably worn out" Bronse Bushings at the trans outputs. These parts are available from "The Fiero Store" website and cost $79.00 US for the pair. These parts are designed to be pressed in, no modification necessary.<P>If nothing else, you should look closely at the bushings when you replace the seals, if they are really worn, that's probably want took the seals out, because the output shaft is wobbling (sometimes the seals just plane dry up and a rubber seal rejuvinator liquid that you add to the trans oil will take care of the problem. If I recall correctly I think STP made the stuff, worked on my car for 1.5 years, right up to selling it, and may be still OK ?).<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||JayUSA77||07-18-2001||12:08 AM||||GOOD LUCK i hate axle seals damnit.  I changed my clutch in my 88 fiero 4cyl and the thing that took me the longest was putting new axle seals in without the proper tools.  I HATE AXLE SEALS. just thought i would like to share that with you. Oh ya GOOD LUCK <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>j<P>------------------<BR>86' Black Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||Joe Torma||07-18-2001||12:32 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by California Kid:<BR><B>If you plan on keeping that car a long time, you should try to get you money back for the seals and get the Axle Stabilizer Needle Bearing with Seal build in. This also replaces the "probably worn out" Bronse Bushings at the trans outputs. These parts are available from "The Fiero Store" website and cost $79.00 US for the pair. These parts are designed to be pressed in, no modification necessary.<BR>?).</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's what I have...and one of mine leaks(metal to metal on the trans).  it stinks.  I know I'm going to break/ruin the trans if I try to take it out.<BR>Hopefully the silicone will work.<P>joe<P>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Steve Normington||07-18-2001||12:42 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Joe Torma:<BR><B> That's what I have...and one of mine leaks(metal to metal on the trans).  it stinks.  I know I'm going to break/ruin the trans if I try to take it out.<BR>Hopefully the silicone will work.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Are you my clone? I have the same problem. You'll need something thin like a Popsicle™ stick to spread the silicone in the small gap between the axle seal and the transmission.<P>PS. I drained the oil and cleaned the area with Simple Green™ to get all the oil off.||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||07-18-2001||08:29 AM||||The "proper" (?) tools I used, were a 3-4" piece of 2-1/2" (?) PVC pipe, and a 2-lb sledge hammer.  And many restarts.<P>Even though I don't replace axle seals every day, it's enough of a pain that I probably wouldn't mind purchasing / making a special tool for installing the seals.  Any ideas? ||||reg||1
